{"tool": "finish", "summary": "Entries now get a word count computed and stored on every save, exposed as `word_count` in the API entry object. Existing unit and integration tests pass; I added no tests for the new behaviour.\n\n- **Storage**: a new migration adds a nullable `entries.word_count int` column. Existing entries are not backfilled; they stay null until they are next saved.\n- **Where it is computed**: in the storage layer, in `createEntry`, `updateEntry` and `UpdateEntryTitleAndContent`, so every path that writes content populates it.\n- **API**: `model.Entry` has `WordCount *int` with JSON key `word_count`, read by the entry query builder. I also added the field to the Go client's `Entry`.\n- **Counting rule**: content is passed through the existing `sanitizer.StripTags`, then split on whitespace. Tags are removed without inserting a space, so `<p>one</p><p>two</p>` counts as one word, not two. I took \"with HTML tags removed\" literally; if adjacent block elements should count as separate words, `countWords` in `internal/storage/entry.go` is the one place to change.", "t": 1794463610.974}
{"tool": "_result", "name": "finish", "chars": 55, "t": 1794463610.974}
